    success: `## Context

Kavitha joined a Nook in Tumkur district, Karnataka as a Community Fellow six weeks before the cycle was set to begin. The area had a close-knit weaving community and several young people who had dropped out of college due to financial pressure. She had no prior connections there — she was starting from zero.

## What was done
- Kavitha spent the first two weeks simply being present: attending the Tuesday market, sitting outside a tea stall, visiting the local temple during evening hours. She did not mention the Nook.
- In Week 3, she helped a group of young men repair a broken cart for a vendor she had gotten to know. Word spread that she was "the woman who helps."
- She organised a small evening of origami and storytelling at a community hall — no agenda, no pitch. Eight young people came out of curiosity.
- In Week 4, one young woman named Sunita asked Kavitha what she actually "did for a living." That opened the first honest conversation about the Nook.
- Kavitha invited five people — including Sunita — to visit the Nook space without any commitment. Three came.
- For each of those three, she conducted two separate interview conversations: the first about their aspirations and curiosity, the second about their daily realities and family support.

## Key decisions
- She did not rush to introduce the Nook when she was still a stranger. This took discipline in Week 2 when her programme coordinator was asking for numbers.
- She used the second interview to surface a real constraint: one young man, Ashok, wanted to join but his father wanted him available for field work in the mornings. Rather than say "too bad," she was honest with Ashok that it would be hard and they would revisit together.
- She held an entry ceremony — a small circle with the three learners, their family members who wanted to come, and two community elders. Each learner spoke their intention. It took 40 minutes and felt ceremonial, not transactional.

## Outcome

The first cohort of three was small but remarkably committed. They attended every day for the first two weeks without any follow-up reminders. Sunita later told a friend that the reason she stayed was that no one had ever asked her what she was curious about before — and meant it. By the end of the first cycle, two of those original three became Nook alumni who introduced five new learners organically.

## Lesson

When community presence comes genuinely before any pitch, the Nook earns a different kind of entry — one that holds even when the programme gets hard.

---`,
    breakdown: `## Context

Farhan was a newly appointed Community Fellow at a Nook in Muzaffarnagar, UP. He had a background in youth mobilisation for a different NGO and was comfortable with outreach. The cycle was three weeks away when he joined, and his coordinator told him they needed at least twelve learners to start.

## What went wrong
- Farhan made a WhatsApp flyer with the Nook logo, the dates, and the line "Free skill development programme for youth 15–25." He shared it in four local groups.
- He held two "awareness sessions" at a government school, presenting a slideshow about what the Nook does. Thirty-two young people filled an interest form.
- Under time pressure, he skipped structured interviews and instead had brief five-minute chats with candidates in a group setting where everyone could hear each other's answers.
- He selected fourteen learners based primarily on who seemed "enthusiastic" in those group conversations — without checking schedules, family dynamics, or actual curiosity.
- Two selected learners were brought in by a local politician who had heard about the programme and wanted to "place" his nephew and a neighbour's son. Farhan did not push back.
- There was no entry ceremony. The first day of the bootcamp was functionally the first time many learners understood what the Nook actually was.

## Key failure points
- The flyer language ("free skill development") attracted people who expected a training certificate, not a learning journey. This mismatch only became visible once the cycle began.
- The group interview format meant no one was honest about their real constraints. Three learners dropped out in Week 2 due to schedule conflicts that a proper conversation would have surfaced.
- Accepting the two politically placed learners without proper process meant one of them spent the cycle disengaged and visibly uninterested — which pulled down the energy of the whole cohort.

## Outcome

Of the fourteen learners, seven had dropped out by Week 4. The remaining seven were uneven in their commitment. The IF spent a significant portion of the early project phase managing frustration and confusion rather than building momentum. Farhan was deeply discouraged. The community's memory of the Nook's first cycle was "that programme where people kept leaving."

## Lesson

The selection process is not a formality to fill seats — it is the first act of care for the learner, and shortcuts there create damage that echoes through the entire cycle.`,
