    breakdown: `## Context

Prashant and Divya are selected for the new Nook in Osmanabad, Maharashtra. Both are strong candidates individually. Prashant has deep community connections in the area; Divya has facilitation training and real energy. DEFY's program team is managing five onboardings that quarter and is stretched thin. They compress the training from six weeks to three.

## What went wrong
- Prashant spends four days at the host Nook in Pune before being called back for a family emergency. He never completes his observation period. The program team decides he's "a community person — he doesn't really need the immersion."
- No pairing conversation is ever formally held. Prashant and Divya naturally settle into a dynamic: he handles all community relationships, she handles all session facilitation. Nobody names this as a risk.
- The co-Fellow calibration session is replaced with a 45-minute call the night before Cycle 1 begins. The four pairing questions are emailed in a document that neither Fellow refers to again.
- By Month 3, Divya is running circles and project sessions alone because Prashant's community work keeps him out of the Nook until late afternoon. She hasn't named this to anyone.
- The Month 4 coaching call is 25 minutes — DEFY is behind on a report. The call covers data. When the program lead asks "How are you two working together?", Prashant says "Fine" and Divya says "Good." The program lead accepts this and moves on.
- By Month 5, learners are privately complaining to Prashant that Divya doesn't know the community. A few older learners have started showing up only when Prashant is present.

## Key failure points
- When Prashant's training was cut short, the program team had enough information to pause — they chose not to because they were behind schedule. That was the moment where a course correction was still easy.
- When Divya and Prashant separated into domains without naming it, a brief check-in at any coaching call would have surfaced it. But no coaching call ever asked about the co-Fellow relationship specifically.
- At Month 4, the coaching call felt like a checkpoint rather than a conversation. The question "How are you two working together?" invited a two-word answer and got one.

## Outcome

Cycle 1 ends without a formal mid-term review. Both Fellows complete their documentation, the exhibition happens, and the Nook closes the cycle. But the community has two informal groups now — Prashant's people and the rest. When Divya leaves after Cycle 2, three learners disengage within a month. The Nook does not collapse, but it takes a cycle and a half to recover something like its original breadth.

## Lesson

A compressed onboarding and an unexamined co-Fellow dynamic do not surface as problems until they're expensive to fix — which is why the six-week minimum and the pairing conversation exist.`,
